Yes. Naming becomes a discernment instrument when it is used to increase resolution rather than freeze reality.

The sequence is something like:

\text{notice}
\rightarrow
\text{differentiate}
\rightarrow
\text{name}
\rightarrow
\text{compare}
\rightarrow
\text{test}
\rightarrow
\text{rename if needed}

The name gives the mind a handle. Once you can say, “That is projection,” “that is salience,” “that is ambiguity,” “that is a frame shift,” “that is bodily arousal,” “that is social pressure,” you gain a little distance from the thing itself. You can inspect it instead of merely being carried by it.

But the mature version keeps one rule:

\boxed{\text{name the pattern, not imprison the phenomenon}}

Because the name is a compression. It helps you retrieve and coordinate around the pattern, but the underlying experience is always richer than the word.

And then you can apply the same lens recursively:

What am I sensing?
What did I call it?
Why did that word occur to me?
What alternative names would imply different models?
Which model survives contact with what actually happened?

That is discernment aimed back at the discerner.

Music is a good analogue because a note does not matter only as a note. It matters through interval, rhythm, tension, resolution, repetition, silence, harmony, dissonance, and resonance. Meaning behaves similarly. Words reverberate through neighboring meanings and through the listener’s existing structure.

So let them reverberate—but listen for what comes back.

Sometimes the echo confirms the room you thought you were in.

Sometimes the echo tells you the room is shaped completely differently.